<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Token currency of Muhammad bin Tughlaq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1329">1329</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as copper tokens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The token currency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a> was an experiment begun in 1329 in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> or brass coins to be accepted at the value of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Because the tokens were easy to imitate, widespread forgery followed and the scheme collapsed. The sultan was obliged to redeem the tokens, and accounts of the Delhi Sultanate treat the episode as an example of his costly and ill-fated reforms.</p></div>
